{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0916"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c077): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c077.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0916"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge film-media/film","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/film-media__film.g5.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",66],["+","Sometimes it was not even the film but a single image. In the Hammer production One Million Years B.C. (1966), Raquel Welch played the Stone Age woman Loana and spoke almost no dialogue; what audiences remembered was her costume, a fur bikini of animal hide. "],["=",2],["+","publicity still of Welch in it became a best-selling poster and turned her into one of the defining sex symbols of the 1960s.\n\nThe "],["=",830],["+","A year later Rachel McAdams had her own double breakthrough: the teen comedy Mean Girls and the tearjerker The Notebook (2004), based on Nicholas Sparks's novel. Off camera the start was rocky – McAdams and her co-star Ryan Gosling did not get along at first and clashed openly on set – yet their on-screen romance made the film a sleeper hit, and the two later became a couple in real life.\n\n"],["=",139],["+","\n| Rachel McAdams | The Notebook | Nick Cassavetes | 2004 |"],["=",7],["-","five "],["=",37]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",70],["+","Manchmal war es nicht einmal der Film, sondern ein einziges Bild. In der Hammer-Produktion „Eine Million Jahre vor unserer Zeit“ (1966) spielte Raquel Welch die Steinzeitfrau Loana und sprach kaum ein Wort; in Erinnerung blieb ihr Kostüm, ein Fellbikini aus Tierhaut. Das Werbefoto von Welch darin wurde ein Bestseller-Poster und machte sie zu einem der prägenden Sexsymbole der 1960er-Jahre.\n\n"],["=",852],["+","Ein Jahr später erlebte Rachel McAdams gleich einen doppelten Durchbruch: mit der Teeniekomödie „Girls Club – Vorsicht bissig!“ und mit der Schnulze Wie ein einziger Tag (2004, im Original „The Notebook“) nach dem Roman von Nicholas Sparks. Hinter der Kamera begann es holprig – McAdams und ihr Partner Ryan Gosling kamen zunächst nicht miteinander aus und gerieten am Set offen aneinander –, doch ihre Leinwandromanze machte den Film zum Überraschungserfolg, und später wurden die beiden auch im echten Leben ein Paar.\n\n"],["=",145],["+","\n| Rachel McAdams | Wie ein einziger Tag | Nick Cassavetes | 2004 |"],["=",5],["-","fünf "],["=",35]]}]},
